audio_mem: let the shared-memory addresses move with the image

An AGDSP image has its addresses compiled in, and UMS9620 boards do not place them
alike. This board's own audio-mem-mgr puts audio-mem at 0xaf700000 and cmdaddr at
0xaf980000; a Moto G35, the same T760, puts them at 0xaf600000 and 0xaf880000 - a
megabyte lower, with a 7 MiB DSP region instead of 6. Loading an image built for
one layout at the other runs it into memory that belongs to something else, which
on this board takes the whole device down rather than failing quietly.

ddr32_base/dspbin_base already move the two regions, and the sub-areas carved out
of them (the DMA area, the dump area) follow ddr32_base on their own because the
driver derives those from res.start. What does not follow is cmdaddr, smsg-addr
and the shmaddr-* properties: those are absolute in the device tree and would
still point at this board's layout.

shm_shift subtracts a constant from exactly those, so one reservation covering
both layouts plus the right parameters is enough to try an image from another
board. Left at zero nothing changes.

This applies on top of audio-mem-fixed-region.patch, which is what introduces
the module parameters it sits next to. Generate it against that base and not
against a tree that already carries a draft of this one, or the declaration ends
up as context instead of an addition and the result compiles the helper before
the variable it uses.

The call goes in audio_mem_sharkl5_parse_dt and not in the whale2 one next to
it: this driver has three parsers and the board's node is
compatible = "unisoc,audio-mem-sharkl5". Patching the wrong one compiles, loads,
accepts the parameter and does nothing at all, which is a quiet way to lose an
experiment.

--- a/sprd_audio/audiomem/audio_mem.c
+++ b/sprd_audio/audiomem/audio_mem.c
--- a/sprd_audio/audiomem/audio_mem.c
+++ b/sprd_audio/audiomem/audio_mem.c
@@ -484,6 +484,36 @@
 module_param(dspbin_size, uint, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dspbin_size, "size of dspbin_base, in bytes");
 
+/*
+ * An AGDSP image carries its addresses built in, and UMS9620 boards do not agree on them: a Moto G35 puts
+ * audio-mem at 0xaf600000 and cmdaddr at 0xaf880000, a megabyte below where this board's device tree puts
+ * the same things. The sub-areas carved out of the reserved region follow ddr32_base on their own, but
+ * cmdaddr, smsg-addr and the shmaddr-* properties are absolute in the device tree, so they have to be moved
+ * as well before an image built for the other layout can run.
+ */
+static unsigned int shm_shift;
+module_param(shm_shift, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(shm_shift, "subtract this from the device tree's absolute shared-memory addresses");
+
+static void audio_mem_apply_shm_shift(void)
+{
+	static const int shifted[] = {
+		IRAM_SMS_COMMD_PARAMS, IRAM_SMS, IRAM_SHM_AUD_STR,
+		IRAM_SHM_DSP_VBC, IRAM_SHM_NXP, IRAM_SHM_REG_DUMP,
+	};
+	int i;
+
+	if (!shm_shift)
+		return;
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(shifted); i++) {
+		if (!audio_mem[shifted[i]].addr)
+			continue;
+		audio_mem[shifted[i]].addr -= shm_shift;
+	}
+	pr_info("%s: moved the shared-memory addresses down by %#x, cmdaddr is now %#x\n",
+		__func__, shm_shift, audio_mem[IRAM_SMS_COMMD_PARAMS].addr);
+}
+
 static int audio_mem_region(struct device_node *np, int index, struct resource *res)
 {
 	struct device_node *memnp;
@@ -992,6 +1022,8 @@
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
+	audio_mem_apply_shm_shift();
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
